{"cuisine/inuit-diet":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/inuit-diet","n":"Inuit cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Historically, Inuit cuisine, which is taken here to include the Greenlandic, the Yupʼik and Aleut cuisines, consisted of a diet of animal source foods that were fished, hunted, and gathered locally."],["After hunting, they often honour the animals' spirit by singing songs and performing rituals. Although traditional or country foods still play an important role in the identity of Inuit, much food is purchased from the store, which has led to health problems and food insecurity. According to Edmund Searles in his article Food and the Making of Modern Inuit Identities, they consume this type of diet because a mostly meat diet is \"effective in keeping the body warm, making the body strong, keeping the body fit, and even making that body healthy\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"inuit-diet"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inuit_diet"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Niqiliurniq","ref":"cookbook/niqiliurniq","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Micah Arreak, Annie Désilets, Lucy Kappianaq, Glenda Kripanik and Kanadaise Uyarasuk, 2019","note":"Inuktitut and English cookbook of Inuit cooking from Nunavut, published by Inhabit Media.","url":"https://49thshelf.com/Books/N/Niqiliurniq","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Muktuk","dish/muktuk"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Whale","ingredient/whale-meat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Inuit cooking","count":1,"links":[["Iñupiaq foodways","cuisine/inupiaq-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/47/Maktaaq_2_2002-08-10.jpg/500px-Maktaaq_2_2002-08-10.jpg","w":500,"h":342,"by":"Ansgar Walk","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maktaaq_2_2002-08-10.jpg"}},"cuisine/tajik-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tajik-cuisine","n":"Tajik cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Tajikistan","intro":[["Tajik cuisine is a traditional cuisine of Tajikistan, and has much in common with Iranian, Afghan, Russian, Chinese,  and Uzbek cuisines. Palov or palav (pilaf) (Tajik: палав), also called osh (ash) (Tajik: ош), is the national dish in Tajikistan, as in other countries in the region. Green tea (Tajik: чойи кабуд) is the common national drink."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tajik-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tajik_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Red Sands: Reportage and Recipes Through Central Asia, from Hinterland to Heartland","ref":"cookbook/red-sands-reportage-and-recipes-through-central-asia-from-hinterland-to-heartland","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Caroline Eden, 2020","note":"Travel writing and recipes across the five Central Asian republics, not this cuisine alone.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":6,"links":[["Chorba","dish/chorba"],["Mastava","dish/mastava"],["Nisholda","dish/nisholda"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Qurutob","dish/qurutob"],["Shakarap","dish/shakarap"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Beans","ingredient/beans"],["Legumes","ingredient/legumes"],["Vegetables","ingredient/vegetables"],["Citric acid","ingredient/citric-acid"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Stock (cooking)","ingredient/stock-cooking"],["Kashk","ingredient/kashk"],["Dill","ingredient/dill"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b8/Tajik_dastarkhan_meal.jpg/500px-Tajik_dastarkhan_meal.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"I, Ibrahimjon","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tajik_dastarkhan_meal.jpg"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-sardinia":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-sardinia","n":"Sardinian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["The cuisine of Sardinia is the traditional cuisine of the island of Sardinia. It is characterised by its variety and by the fact of having been enriched through a number of interactions with the other Mediterranean cultures while retaining its own identity."],["Sardinia's food culture is strictly divided into food from the land and food from the sea, reflecting the island's history and geography. Sardinian cuisine is considered part of the Mediterranean diet, a nutritional model that was proclaimed by UNESCO as an intangible cultural heritage."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-sardinia"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Sardinia"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The Sardinian Cookbook: The Cooking and Culture of a Mediterranean Island","ref":"cookbook/the-sardinian-cookbook-the-cooking-and-culture-of-a-mediterranean-island","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Viktorija Todorovska","note":"Introduction to Sardinian food culture with traditional recipes.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"A Sardinian Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/a-sardinian-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Giovanni Pilu, Roberta Muir","note":"Sardinian recipes from a Sardinian-born chef working in Sydney.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Fregula","dish/fregula"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ea/Olmedo_-_Pani_artistici_%2817%29.jpg/500px-Olmedo_-_Pani_artistici_%2817%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Gianni Careddu","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmedo_-_Pani_artistici_(17).jpg"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-dorset":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-dorset","n":"Dorset cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United Kingdom","intro":[["The cuisine of Dorset, a county in South West England, is characterised by a variety of, often simple, dishes which are sourced, or have traditionally been sourced, from the county itself. Today, the cuisine of Dorset reflects the wider shift towards organic and local produce. These principles are upheld by farmers' markets, farm shops and fairs held across Dorset."],["Many of the traditional dishes originate from the 17th and 18th centuries. At this time, much of the workforce were employed as agricultural labourers and, though they produced most of the food, their diet was poor. The gentry, unique in being able to shop for produce, and farmers, fared better. Despite this, until the early 19th century at least, simple foods, such as bread, comprised a sizeable part of the diet of all classes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-dorset"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Dorset"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"ingredient/wheat":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/wheat","n":"Wheat","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 11 dishes across 26 cuisines","intro":[["Wheat is a group of wild and domesticated grasses of the genus Triticum (). As cereals, they are cultivated for their grains, which are staple foods around the world. Well-known wheat species and hybrids include the most widely grown common wheat (T. aestivum), spelt, durum, emmer, einkorn, and Khorasan or Kamut. The archaeological record suggests that wheat was first cultivated in the regions of the Fertile Crescent around 9600 BC."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-wheat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":26,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Blini","dish/blini"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Crozets de Savoie","dish/crozets-de-savoie"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Crozets de Savoie","dish/crozets-de-savoie"]]],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Kasha","dish/kasha"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Kasha","dish/kasha"]]],["Tunisian cuisine","cuisine/tunisian-cuisine",[["Bazin (bread)","dish/bazin-bread"],["Couscous","dish/couscous"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Kasha","dish/kasha"]]],["Algerian cuisine","cuisine/algerian-cuisine",[["Co