","dish/paensae"],["Pyeonsu","dish/pyeonsu"],["Pyongyang Raengmyon","dish/pyongyang-raengmyon"],["Sungeo-guk","dish/sungeo-guk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/85/Okryu_Restaurant_in_Pyongyang.jpg/500px-Okryu_Restaurant_in_Pyongyang.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Kounosu","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okryu_Restaurant_in_Pyongyang.jpg"}},"dish/raclette":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/raclette","n":"Raclette","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Swiss cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Raclette is a dish of Swiss origin, also popular in other countries, based on heating cheese and scraping off the melted part, then typically served with boiled potatoes. Raclette is historically a dish originating from the canton of Valais in Switzerland. This cheese from Valais benefits from an AOP. Raclette cheese is also a Swiss-type cheese marketed specifically to be used for this dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-raclette"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raclette"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Raclette du Valais","ingredient/raclette-du-valais"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Helvetic Kitchen","ref":"site/helvetickitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"helvetickitchen.com","note":"Andie Pilot's blog of Swiss home cooking and baking, with notes on Swiss cookbooks.","url":"https://www.helvetickitchen.com/recipes/retrospective","suggested":true},{"name":"Cuisine Helvetica","ref":"site/cuisinehelvetica-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cuisinehelvetica.com","note":"Heddi Nieuwsma's Swiss recipes and food culture from her life in Switzerland.","url":"https://cuisinehelvetica.com/swiss-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Swiss cuisine","links":[["Älplermagronen","dish/alplermagronen"],["Basler Läckerli","dish/basler-lackerli"],["Birnbrot","dish/birnbrot"],["Bündnerfleisch","dish/bundnerfleisch"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Carac (pastry)","dish/carac-pastry"],["Cervelat","dish/cervelat"],["Cholera (food)","dish/cholera-food"],["Egg puffs and soup pearls","dish/egg-puffs-and-soup-pearls"],["Landjäger","dish/landjager"],["Leberkäse","dish/leberkase"],["Riz Casimir","dish/riz-casimir"],["Schenkele","dish/schenkele"],["Spätzle","dish/spatzle"],["Swiss sausages and cured meats","dish/swiss-sausages-and-cured-meats"],["Wähe","dish/wahe"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"],["Zuger Kirschtorte","dish/zuger-kirschtorte"],["Zürcher Geschnetzeltes","dish/zurcher-geschnetzeltes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2f/Raclette_20040817_140816.jpg/500px-Raclette_20040817_140816.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"Ka23 13","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raclette_20040817_140816.jpg"}},"dish/riz-casimir":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/riz-casimir","n":"Riz Casimir","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Swiss cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Riz Casimir (from French, sometimes Casimir Rice) is a curry dish originating in Switzerland. It is made from rice, sliced veal, curry sauce, and fruits, usually bananas, pineapples and peaches or cherries. It is also garnished with roasted almond slivers. Long-grain rice is normally used for Riz Casimir. Veal is often replaced with pork or chicken. Other fruits may also be used."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-riz-casimir"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riz_Casimir"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Banana","ingredient/banana"],["Curry powder","ingredient/curry-powder"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Helvetic Kitchen","ref":"site/helvetickitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"helvetickitchen.com","note":"Andie Pilot's blog of Swiss home cooking and baking, with notes on Swiss cookbooks.","url":"https://www.helvetickitchen.com/recipes/retrospective","suggested":true},{"name":"Cuisine Helvetica","ref":"site/cuisinehelvetica-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cuisinehelvetica.com","note":"Heddi Nieuwsma's Swiss recipes and food culture from her life in Switzerland.","url":"https://cuisinehelvetica.com/swiss-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Swiss cuisine","links":[["Älplermagronen","dish/alplermagronen"],["Basler Läckerli","dish/basler-lackerli"],["Birnbrot","dish/birnbrot"],["Bündnerfleisch","dish/bundnerfleisch"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Carac (pastry)","dish/carac-pastry"],["Cervelat","dish/cervelat"],["Cholera (food)","dish/cholera-food"],["Egg puffs and soup pearls","dish/egg-puffs-and-soup-pearls"],["Landjäger","dish/landjager"],["Leberkäse","dish/leberkase"],["Raclette","dish/raclette"],["Schenkele","dish/schenkele"],["Spätzle","dish/spatzle"],["Swiss sausages and cured meats","dish/swiss-sausages-and-cured-meats"],["Wähe","dish/wahe"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"],["Zuger Kirschtorte","dish/zuger-kirschtorte"],["Zürcher Geschnetzeltes","dish/zurcher-geschnetzeltes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/3/3b/Riz_Casimir_%284247740294%29.jpg","w":500,"h":325,"by":"kochtopf","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riz_Casimir_(4247740294).jpg"}},"ingredient/pulse-legume":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pulse-legume","n":"Pulse (legume)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Legumes are plants in the pea family Fabaceae (or Leguminosae), or the fruit or seeds of such plants. When used as a dry grain for human consumption, the seeds are also called pulses. Legumes are grown agriculturally, primarily for human consumption, but also as livestock forage and silage, and as soil-enhancing green manure. Legumes produce a botanically unique type of fruit—a simple dry fruit that develops from a simple carpel and usually dehisces (opens along a seam) on two sides."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pulse-legume"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pulse_(legume)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Scotch broth","dish/scotch-broth"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Rutabaga","ingredient/rutabaga"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e7/Various_legumes.jpg/500px-Various_legumes.jpg","w":500,"h":748,"by":"Keith Weller, Agricultural Research Service, USDA","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Various_legumes.jpg"}},"dish/strong-beer":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/strong-beer","n":"Strong beer","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Strong beer is a term for beer that is in some way more potent than regular beer. The concept is only generally used in some countries, and local tax laws often define what constitutes strong beer."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-strong-beer"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strong_beer"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine"],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scandicuisine: Swedish recipes","ref":"site/scandicuisine-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scandicuisine.com","note":"Traditional Swedish recipes from Cecilia, a cook from a Finnish-Swedish fam